    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p9-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find a formula for the sum,\n$$1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1).$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n^2$.",
          "worked": "There are $n$ terms, with first term $1$ and last term $2n-1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}[1+(2n-1)]\\\\S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(2n)\\\\S_n&=n^2.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Use your formula to evaluate\n$$1+3+\\cdots+99.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2500$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2n-1&=99\\\\2n&=100\\\\n&=50.\\end{align*}$$\nUsing part (a),\n$$\\begin{align*}S_{50}&=50^2\\\\S_{50}&=2500.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 27
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find $n$ for which\n$$1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1)=10\\,000.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=100$.",
          "worked": "Using part (a),\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2&=10\\,000\\\\n&=100.\\end{align*}$$\nTake the positive root because $n$ counts the terms.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 19
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q7-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "In 1615, the following interesting pattern was observed by Galileo:\n$$\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1+3}{5+7}=\\frac{1+3+5}{7+9+11}.$$\nProve that this pattern continues.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\frac{1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1)}{(2n+1)+(2n+3)+\\cdots+(4n-1)}=\\frac13$ for every positive integer $n$.\n\nThe numerator is $n^2$ and the denominator is $(2n)^2-n^2=3n^2$.",
          "worked": "Let $N_n$ be the sum of the first $n$ odd positive integers and $D_n$ the sum of the next $n$. By part (a),\n$$N_n=n^2.$$\nThe first $2n$ odd positive integers have sum $(2n)^2$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}D_n&=(2n)^2-n^2\\\\D_n&=4n^2-n^2\\\\D_n&=3n^2.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{N_n}{D_n}&=\\frac{n^2}{3n^2}\\\\\\frac{N_n}{D_n}&=\\frac13.\\end{align*}$$\nThis holds for every positive integer $n$, so the pattern continues.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      }
    ]
  },
